  Cell["Reading guide", "Subtitle"], Cell["This annotated notebook explains \
the existing calculations step by step. Read each text cell before evaluating \
the following Input cell. All original Input and Output cells are preserved, \
including the original saved results; they have not been recomputed or \
certified by this editorial update. Use a fresh kernel when rerunning the \
notebook. Notes flag notation and algebraic issues that should be addressed \
in a later code revision.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Boundary cases and what remains to be derived", "Section"], 
  Cell["Production: the continuous extension of the Cobb\[Dash]Douglas \
function is zero when any essential input is zero. With the other inputs \
positive and fixed, each marginal product tends to infinity as its own input \
approaches zero from above and tends to zero as its own input tends to \
infinity. These are one-variable Inada limits; a limit in which several \
inputs change together need not have the same behaviour. The interior \
derivative formulas and logarithms should not be evaluated directly at a zero \
input.", "Text"], Cell["Optimality: the technology is concave and has \
constant returns to scale. With all factor prices given and the output price \
normalised to one, the firm's first-order conditions do not uniquely \
determine its scale. If the unit production cost is above one, the firm \
chooses zero output; if it equals one, every scale has zero variable profit; \
if it is below one, unrestricted profit is unbounded. Demand, factor-market \
clearing, or another restriction is needed to determine finite production.", 
   "Text"], Cell["CES boundaries: \[Sigma]=1 must be handled through the \
Cobb\[Dash]Douglas limit, and \[Gamma]=1 through logarithmic utility. A \
positive CES weight on energy does not always make energy essential: for \
\[Sigma]>1, composite consumption can remain positive when household energy \
is zero; for 0<\[Sigma]\[LessEqual]1, the continuous extension gives zero \
composite consumption when either good is absent. The multiplier-elimination \
argument in this notebook concerns positive quantities and finite positive \
prices, not corner solutions.", "Text"], Cell["Capital accumulation: the \
original final section contains no calculation. A standard next step is \
K[t+1]=(1\[Minus]\[Delta])K[t]+I[t], with 0\[LessEqual]\[Delta]\[LessEqual]1 \
and a specified timing convention. Deriving optimal saving or investment \
would also require its budget treatment, an Euler equation, and appropriate \
no-Ponzi or transversality conditions. These intertemporal conditions are not \
implied by the static firm FOCs or the within-period CES allocation derived \
here.", "Text"]}, WindowSize -> {1428, 729.75}, 
 WindowMargins -> {{0, Automatic}, {Automatic, 0}}, 
